**=================================================================================================
** Bonded anchor pry-out test -- EXPLICIT DYNAMIC (NED) variant of test.inp
**=================================================================================================
**
** Derived from test.inp (implicit, NISTParallel + blockamg) by the changes recorded in
** PLAN_EXPLICIT_PRYOUT.md section 4. Read that document before changing anything here; several of
** the values below are not free parameters but consequences of the explicit time increment.
**
** CONSISTENT UNITS: N, mm, MPa, tonne, s.
**
** MASS SCALING: every density is 10x its physical value (concrete 3e-8 instead of 3e-9, steel 7.85e-8,
** mortar 2.2e-8). The stable time increment goes with 1/sqrt(rho), so this buys sqrt(10) = 3.16x and
** takes the run from roughly 9.5 h to 3 h. The cost is that inertial forces at a given loading rate
** are 10x larger, which works AGAINST quasi-staticity -- so the kinetic energy the journal reports and
** the RF_loading/U_loading CSVs (whose integral is the external work) are what decide whether the
** result is usable, not the fact that it completed. Divide the reported kinetic energy by 10 to
** compare against an unscaled run.
**
** INITIAL REFINEMENT ONLY. The mesh is refined exactly once, before the increment loop, over a
** pry-out-cone ("chalice") shaped domain about the anchor axis, and is then FIXED for the whole
** analysis. This is the deliberate difference from run_production_v13, which used a live principal-
** stress marker and refined 67 times over 20 h. Two reasons, one physical and one an outright bug:
**
**   - Every mid-run refinement drops a fresh hanging-node transition into an already-damaged stress
**     field, so a damage pattern near a transition cannot be separated from the transition itself.
**     Here there is one transition, it is placed outside any credible breakout cone, and it exists
**     from increment 0, i.e. as part of the initial condition rather than as an event.
**   - MEASURED on run_production_v13's own output: after the first live refinement, EVERY Ensight
**     variable file is shorter than the geometry part it is painted onto (at frame 65, 14536 damage
**     values and 66832 nodal values against a part with 18820 elements and 86640 nodes). The
**     exported damage and displacement fields of that run are therefore not aligned with the mesh
**     at all. Do not draw conclusions from its frames. With the mesh fixed after step start this
**     cannot arise, and the writer's own size guard is left ENABLED here as the tripwire.
**
** WHAT CHANGED, and why:
**
**  1. Densities. Required now: the solver builds a lumped mass from getDensity() on every element.
**     Concrete 3e-9, steel 7.85e-9, mortar/grout 2.2e-9 t/mm^3. The concrete value was already in
**     the file but was NOT being read -- GCDP's getDensity() indexed the same property slot
**     computeStress() reads the Duvaut-Lions viscosity from, so it returned 1e-6 and the concrete
**     came out 333x too heavy. Fixed in the GCDP module (fix/density-property-index); the property
**     list below is now 21 entries, density at idx 19 and nonlocal viscosity at idx 20.
**
**  2. The Duvaut-Lions viscosity is ZERO here, down from 1e-6. It is a relaxation time. At an
**     implicit increment of ~5e-3 s, dT/eta ~ 5000 and it acts as mild numerical regularisation; at
**     the explicit increment of ~2e-7 s, dT/eta = 0.2 means almost nothing relaxes per increment and
**     the concrete responds very nearly ELASTICALLY -- a run that completes, looks plausible, and
**     shows no softening. Explicit integration needs no Newton to stabilise, so zero is right.
**
**  3. The nonlocal viscosity (new, idx 20) is the mass of the nonlocal damage field's forward-Euler
**     update -- it is what makes an elliptic regularisation field explicitly integrable. It also
**     sets that field's own stability limit, roughly dT <~ 2*eta_nl/(1 + l^2/h^2), and NOTHING
**     CHECKS IT: the element's computeCriticalTimeStepForExplicitDynamics carries an explicit
**     "TODO: current implementation ignores nonlocal variables". With l = 2 mm and the post-
**     refinement h = 1.14 mm, l^2/h^2 = 3.1 and dT ~ 2e-7, so the floor is ~4e-7. 1e-5 is ~24x above
**     it. Size this against the FINEST element the run can reach, not the initial mesh.
**
**  4. Solver NEDParallel, with the displacement field on central differences and the nonlocal damage
**     field on forward Euler. No linear solver: an explicit increment solves no system, so
**     blockamg.json is irrelevant here.
**
**  5. stepLength is now PHYSICAL SECONDS, not the implicit model's dimensionless 1. The critical
**     time step is set by the steel, not the concrete -- see point 9 for the audit. maxNumInc, not
**     stepLength, is what bounds a smoke test.
**
**  6. A quintic smoothstep amplitude on the loading Dirichlet, so the plate starts from rest with
**     zero velocity AND zero acceleration. The implicit file's header claimed one but the block had
**     none; under implicit statics that was harmless, here it would shock the structure.
**
**  7. The warm-up step is gone -- it exists to settle tie/contact state for a Newton solve, and a
**     single explicit increment of 2e-7 s settles nothing.
**
**  8. The AMR block carries a single initialOnly elementSet marker over the generated
**     'chalice_refine' set (see make_chalice_elset.py), replacing v13's initialOnly borehole-
**     surface marker AND its live stress marker. The chalice's cylindrical stem (r <= 30 mm) covers
**     the borehole the surface marker used to, so nothing is lost. topology-check-frequency=0 makes
**     the single pre-loop topology update explicit rather than incidental. Note also that an
**     instantaneous-stress threshold is the wrong criterion for an explicit run in any case: the
**     stress carries the full wave content and there is no coarsening anywhere in the code, so it
**     ratchets toward refining the whole element set. See PLAN_LIVE_AMR_EXPLICIT.md section 5.
**
**  9. dt is UNCHANGED from v13 at 5.456791e-08 s (courant 0.1). Audited before the run: the
**     governing element is a 1.25 mm anchor C3D8 at dt_crit = 6.75e-07 (centre-Jacobian estimate;
**     Marmot's min-over-quadrature-points value is the reported 5.4568e-07). The smallest marked
**     concrete element is 2.484 mm, so splitFactor=2 takes it to 1.242 mm and dt_crit ~ 9.5e-07 --
**     still above the anchor. splitFactor=3 would NOT be free: it takes the concrete to ~6.3e-07
**     and the concrete starts governing.
**
** UNCHANGED from test.inp: the mesh, all node/element sets, all 5 ties, both contact pairs, and
** every Dirichlet boundary condition. The simplification notes 1-7 in test.inp's own header still
** apply and are not repeated here.
**
